    <h1 class="font-semibold text-black"> Our Why</h1>

    <p class="text-black mt-4">Across the United States and around the world, youth are struggling with rising rates of anxiety, depression, low self-worth, and a lack of access to trusted adults who can support their emotional development. In fact, in the US alone, over 1.8 million young adults between 18-24 did not have a mentor in their lives during their childhood and adolescence while also not being able to nominate anyone that they felt was a meaningful person in their lives.
These challenges are especially urgent during the formative elementary and middle school years, when children are building their identities, shaping their worldview, and forming the beliefs they’ll carry into adolescence and adulthood.
In North Carolina, where this initiative will begin, recent data shows that:
•	1 in 3 middle school students report persistent sadness or hopelessness,
•	33% feel alone in life, and
•	nearly half lack strong self-confidence.
While schools provide some support, 39% of students report not having an adult at school they can talk to, and access to consistent, caring adults outside the family is even more limited.
These emotional and relational gaps don’t just impact mental health—they also hold students back from discovering who they are, what they care about, and who they want to become. Without confidence, connection, and encouragement, too many kids struggle to believe they have something meaningful to offer the world.
There is a critical need for another program that supports youth in developing emotional resilience, building strong relationships with mentors, and exploring their identity and purpose. By creating a safe, encouraging environment filled with trusted adults, enriching programs, and peer support, we can help youth not only feel better—but become their best selves and find meaning in who they are becoming. Because they were always chosen for more.
</p>

